-- title:
--   Clearing denominators in integrality over ℚ[t]
-- statement:
--   Let $t$ and $x$ be Laurent series with rational coefficients, i.e. elements of `LaurentSeries ℚ`, and suppose $x$ is integral over the $\mathbb{Q}$-subalgebra $\mathbb{Q}[t]$ of `LaurentSeries ℚ` generated by the single element $t$, that is, $x$ is a root of a monic polynomial whose coefficients lie in `Algebra.adjoin ℚ {t}`. No integrality or other hypothesis is imposed on $t$ itself. The conclusion asserts the existence of an integer $d \neq 0$ such that the product of the image of $d$ under the structure map $\mathbb{Q} \to$ `LaurentSeries ℚ` with $x$, namely $d \cdot x$, is integral over the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra `Algebra.adjoin ℤ {t}`, i.e. over the subring $\mathbb{Z}[t]$ of `LaurentSeries ℚ` generated by $t$ over $\mathbb{Z}$. No bound on $d$ is claimed, and the integer produced is not asserted to be canonical in any way.
--
--   This is the denominator-clearing step in the descent of an integral dependence from $\mathbb{Q}[t]$ to $\mathbb{Z}[t]$, used with $t$ a Laurent series such as the $q$-expansion of $j$; some nonzero multiplier is genuinely needed, since for instance a rational constant like $1/2$ is integral over $\mathbb{Q}[t]$ but not over the integrally closed ring $\mathbb{Z}[t] \subseteq \mathbb{Z}((q))$. It is cited in the proof of [`ModularCurve.exists_ne_zero_coeffMap_eq_mul_of_isModPFormFn`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_ne_zero_coeffMap_eq_mul_of_isModPFormFn), where the integer $d$ becomes the bounded denominator of that statement.
